omx-dsp (0.32.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * release 0.32.4: the changelog lists everything merged since v0.32.3;
    floors only
  * curve-json: no separator touched by a comma-decimal locale, duplicate
    keys refused, rt-gate tags, measured tolerances (review of #89)
  * curve: omx_curve_points_json, the curve function with JSON text on both
    sides (lane B4, part 1)
  * curve: the shared vectors, the oracle and the curve-json arm, red until
    omx_curve_points_json exists (lane B4, part 1)
  * docs-check: this clone as the remote, or a base that is HEAD, is refused
    by name (#70)
  * docs-check: the base is fetched and named, never skipped, never the
    head's own copy (#70)
  * fuzz: the three trim records pin the lines this lane's doc blocks moved
    them to (#70)
  * docs: every instance face's run and clear state their parameters; a doc
    block binds to its function (#70)
  * tools: face-conformance reads the resolve definition, not the prose that
    names it (#70)
  * docs: every instance face's init and latency state their parameters and
    returns (#70)
  * fuzz: the three trim records pin the lines omx_trim_instance.h's @file
    block moved them to (#70)
  * docs: omx_delay.h states what three functions return (#70)
  * docs-check: what doxygen cannot see is refused by name before it runs
    (#70)
  * docs: the drive kernel documented in full (#70)
  * docs: the reverb kernel documented in full (#70)
  * docs: {@link fn} becomes fn() across the headers, so every doxygen
    version resolves the same references (#70)
  * docs-check: a missing Doxyfile or baseline, an untracked header and a
    failed git read are refused by name; docs-baseline only lowers (#70)
  * docs: the delay, chorus and flanger kernels documented in full (#70)
  * docs: the delay, chorus, drive and limiter instance faces documented in
    full (#70)
  * tools: install-confined's image file carries its licence (#75)
  * docs: the EQ, compressor and gate instance faces documented in full (#70)
  * docs-check: doxygen's undocumented-declaration warnings against a
    baseline that only shrinks (#70)
  * docs: every installed header carries a @file block, so doxygen sees its
    file-scope declarations (#70)
  * tests: an undocumented public declaration, a grown baseline and a stale
    baseline row must turn make docs-check red (red, #70)
  * tools: install-confined requires every header, sees mode and owner
    changes (#75)
  * tests: one child-run helper for the pool-wrap arm and the hrp_track NULL
    doors
  * tests: the badbound arm comment states the 1..3600 range (#73)
  * tools: flavour-check caps the compile bound at 3600 s and tells a killed
    compiler from a timeout (#73)
  * tests: the compile bound is capped at 3600 s, and a killed compiler is
    not a timeout (red, #73)
  * tests: the pool-wrap arm runs its take in a child, the length through a
    volatile
  * tests: the pool-wrap revert must be a counted failure (red, #71 review)
  * tools: install-confined's image is pinned by digest, declared once (#75)
  * tools: install-confined reads the checkout on an SELinux Enforcing host
    (#75)
  * reverb instance: the clear states why its layout cannot refuse (#72)
  * tests: the reverb clear's layout cannot refuse, and says why (red, #72)
  * tools: install-confined runs in place only on an explicit opt-in, never
    on a container marker (#75)
  * tools: a container marker alone must not start install-confined's inner
    check (red first, #75)
  * tools: flavour-check refuses a bound of zero and reads a killed compile
    as the timeout
  * tests: a zero bound and a compiler that ignores SIGTERM slip past
    flavour-check's timeout (red first)
  * tools: install-confined cannot pass unjudged, runs on a public image and
    in CI (#75)
  * Makefile: the contract directory installs under PREFIX, never the
    system's (#75)
  * tools: make install must stay inside DESTDIR+PREFIX (red first, #75)
  * tests: the stand-in compiler runs the real one without its own directory
    on PATH
  * tests: the stand-in compiler loops through a masquerade wrapper (red
    first)
  * tools: every compile flavour-check starts is bounded, and a timeout is
    red by name
  * tests: flavour-check has no bound on a compiler that never returns (red
    first)
  * hrp track: tag omx_hrp_track_config_valid with a thread the rt gate knows
    (review of #71)
  * reverb: correct the headroom comment, 384 kHz is refused above the
    ceiling (review of #71)
  * hrp render init refuses a track config the tracker refuses (review of #71)
  * test: render init must refuse a track config the tracker refuses (red,
    review of #71)
  * test: omx_pool_take refuses a length whose sum wraps, called directly
    (review of #71)
  * test: reverb layout refuses a rate above the ceiling with a pool that
    fits (review of #71)
  * reverb layout and hrp track update refuse what does not fit (P0 doors)
  * Makefile: TEST_TARGETS is defined before the check-package rule reads it
  * tools: check-package must run make test's targets, not nothing (red first)
  * test: reverb layout and hrp track update must refuse what does not fit
    (red)
  * contract tests: drop the perturbation cases that test the renderer
  * contract tests: drop items-1.3.0
  * contract tests: no test reads an old commit of this repository
  * ci: make test runs in one cell; the contract job needs no history
  * Makefile: CHECK_PACKAGE is derived from make test's list; the checker goes
  * tools: a check that make check-package is derived from make test's list
    (red)
  * debian: drop Breaks and Replaces from omx-dsp-contract
  * contract tests: drop the arms that read old-release fixtures
  * contract-proof: keep completeness, drop the proofs pinned to v0.1.3
  * remove the patch-release API gate
  * checks: red guard, no old-release machinery in the tree
  * data: the time_response docs say "since 0.32.4", the release that ships
    them
  * data: REVERB_DECAY_LOOPS names the curve that reads it
  * fuzz_laws: each sample of a ramp is held to its rule
  * release: the deb job publishes omx-dsp-contract with libomxdsp-dev
  * release: check that the deb job releases every binary a sibling depends on
  * devel package: the rta vectors are in the expected file list
  * test: the models oracle's perturbation arms name the arms that can see
    them
  * twins job: the EQ Q sabotage sizes its jobs from the jobs action
  * drive-instance: the controls helper is tagged audio
  * ci: the trigger gate on every pull request, red
  * ci: the twin-vs-C EQ Q range sabotage runs in the twins job, which has
    node
  * eq-q-range-sabotage: the EQ Q range moves the kernel and the twin
    together, a stale twin goes red
  * ts: the time_response twin, pinned to omx_curve_time by the oracle
  * curve: the time_response of a delay and a reverb, against the kernels
    (red first)
  * curve: the time_response words and the reverb's decay loops, declared
    once (data)
  * curve: CURVE_FAMILIES' doc reads the drive as dB in to dB out
  * curve: omx_curve_drive_sample's doc names its thread, control
  * ts: the drive curve draws the same dB level axis as the C header
  * curve: the drive answers dB in to dB out on the level axis (green)
  * test: the drive's transfer curve is dB in to dB out, against the kernel
    (red)
  * curve: a drive's default grid is a level in dB, -60..0 (data)
  * release 0.32.4: the version in omxdsp.h, the spec, debian/changelog and
    the TypeScript package
  * drive-instance: name resolve in the controls' doc as a link
  * ts: curvePoints draws the drive, the twin of omx_curve_drive
  * curve: the drive family's transfer curve, the kernel's static path
  * test: the drive's transfer curve is what the kernel does (red)
  * curve: the drive family, its five roles and the pc unit (data)
  * drive-instance: the static control mapping as its own inline
  * patch-names-check: a moved count or size macro needs its proof
  * patch-names-check: a moved count or size macro needs its proof (red)
  * flavour-check: the TSan probe says why it cannot read TSan
  * test: flavour-check's TSan probe names its own failure (red)

